## 3. A centered smooth-multiplier contrast lemma

For every smooth scalar F on \(\mathcal Q\), the two actual prebirth states satisfy

\[
 \left|\langle\phi_{1,g}(s),F\phi_{1,g}(s)\rangle
       -\langle\phi_{0,g}(s),F\phi_{0,g}(s)\rangle\right|
 \le C_Tg^2\|F\|_{C^2},\qquad 0\le s\le T.
 \tag{11}
\]

Here C^2 includes the supremum norm, and the statement is uniform for a family of F with bounded C^2 norm. It is not a small trace-distance assertion: vacuum and one-particle states can remain orthogonal.

To prove (11), first use the normalized harmonic reference states from the parent. Before cutoff their probability densities in rescaled coordinates are even under \(x\mapsto-x\). This holds for a complex superposition of one-particle modes at every s: its wavefunction is a complex linear polynomial times the common even Gaussian. Their first moments vanish and their second moments are uniformly finite. After the fixed smooth cutoff, the first moments in the compact chart are exponentially small and the second moments are O(\(g^2\)); the cutoff need not itself be even, because its deviation from one occurs in a Gaussian tail. Each state is normalized, so a constant multiplier cancels exactly in their difference. Taylor's formula for F about the chart origin then gives an O(\(g^2\|F\|_{C^2}\)) difference.

The parent compares each actual unitary orbit to its normalized reference in Hilbert norm by O_T(\(g^2\)) plus an exponentially small term. Since F is bounded, the change of either expectation is at most \(2\|F\|_\infty\) times that norm error. This gives (11) for the actual states. No unbounded operator is applied to the vector-norm error.

Apply (11) to \(\overline G_\theta\) and use (9)-(10). If \(\Delta_{\rm mag}(s,u)\) denotes the difference of the squared norms in (4) with \(e^{uA_g}\) replaced by \(U_{\delta u}\), then

\[
 |\Delta_{\rm mag}(s,u)-\Delta_{\rm mag}(s,0)|
     \le C_T g^2|\theta|
     \le C_T u/\tau_*,
 \quad 0\le\theta\le\theta_0.
 \tag{12}
\]

This is the essential contrast cancellation. Bounding the two magnetic probabilities separately would only give O(\(|\theta|\)) and would lose the improvement. Centering is also essential: an arbitrary displaced preparation can give an O(\(g\|F\|_{C^1}\)) difference instead. No such displaced state is included in this result.

