## 6. Limits, failures preserved and microscopic gap

Smoothness is not by itself a small-g trace-distance estimate. The centered vacuum/one-particle family, its bounded second moments, and the fixed-time approximation to the actual initial orbit are needed in (11). A displaced Gaussian of mean proportional to g can have an O(g) expectation difference on a smooth odd multiplier. The statement is not extended to arbitrary finite-Hermite superpositions with nonzero first moments.

Nor does smoothness give derivative bounds uniform in arbitrarily large \(\theta=\delta u\). The proof uses a fixed bounded magnetic-time interval. The unchanged boundary coefficient cannot be extended by this proof to fixed \(b/g^2>0\), much less to \(b/g^2\to\infty\). In simple smooth matrix models the magnetic coefficient already changes at a nonzero scaled lag. That is an obstruction to inferring an unchanged coefficient from smoothness alone, not a no-go theorem for the particular cubic dynamics. Actual larger-lag behavior could require explicit matrix dynamics or further cancellations not established here.

The full microscopic labeled-process bridge remains separate. For fixed graph, g and positive b, it must compare the event (4) for the original microscopic labeled instrument to the rotor target before shrinking g or b. The spin relation remains
\(\epsilon^2=1/[2g^4S(S+1)]\). Along any proposed joint sequence a sufficient probability error is
\(o(\kappa^2L_Ibg^2)\) for each preparation. No new uniform spin/resource estimate is proved. The previous PRE's finite-grid/register squeeze is still only a route conditional on the appropriate registered theorem. Unconditioned density convergence and a microscopic jump derivative at time zero cannot replace it. No continuum, volume, long-distance, astronomical or empirical limit is taken here.

